# PHP 5.6 — fully pinned. Read the freeze note below before treating
# this base as maintained.
#
# Source: tag 5.6.40 on PHP-5.6-security-backports-openssl11. The Dockerfile
# checks out the immutable commit in PHP_SRC_COMMIT below (that tag peeled),
# never the branch head.
#
# BASE_IMAGE is the registry-reported multi-arch index digest of
# debian:bullseye-slim. Pasted by hand like every base digest: choosing one is
# the same class of security decision as PHP_SRC_COMMIT, so
# tools/resolve-base-digest.sh prints and never writes.
#
# THE BASE IS FROZEN, AND THE DOCKERFILE REWRITES ITS APT SOURCES TO THE ARCHIVE.
# Debian bullseye left its LTS window on 2026-08-31. Its -security index is still
# published at deb.debian.org, but the .debs that index lists 404 there and
# answer 200 only from archive.debian.org/debian-security. The Dockerfile therefore rewrites that one
# suite in /etc/apt/sources.list, keyed on /etc/os-release VERSION_ID=11 and
# asserted with grep, in both apt stages -- the build is reproducible from this
# repository again, and because the rewrite happens in the runtime stage the
# shipped image can apt-get update and install too.
# What it does NOT buy: no further OS security fix, ever. libssl1.1 stays at
# 1.1.1w-0+deb11u8 and libc/icu/curl/libxml2 stay at bullseye's last refresh.
# Accepting that freeze is the price of publishing 5.6 at all; the maintained
# OpenSSL 3 base was measured and declined (see "The 5.6 base is no longer
# maintained..." and "The 5.6 base decision" in manifests/SECURITY-REVIEW.md). One
# residual dependency: deb.debian.org still serves bullseye *main* today. If it
# moves that pool too, the same sed has to cover deb.debian.org/debian.
#
# Route: PHP 5.6 -> openssl11, because the source pin is the branch upstream
# named for it (PHP-5.6-security-backports-openssl11 -- that is where the 5.6.40
# tag points). There is also an untagged PHP-5.6-security-backports branch, and
# it carries the *same* 6-line "Add minimal OpenSSL 3.0 patch": the openssl11
# suffix marks the extra OpenSSL 1.1 (and later 4) work this branch carries, not
# an absence of OpenSSL 3 intent; see "The two PHP 5.6 branches" in
# manifests/SECURITY-REVIEW.md. Neither branch changes the OS: the base image is
# what decides that, and the plain branch is the *older* of the two in library
# terms (0 guards at 0x10100000L, no RSA_get0_key/TLS_client_method -- written
# for OpenSSL 1.0-era APIs). The base must therefore ship OpenSSL 1.1: Debian
# bullseye is at 1.1.1w-0+deb11u8, which is why debian:bullseye-slim is the base
# pinned below. The pin already carries the OpenSSL 3 work (772b292c22df "Add
# minimal OpenSSL 3.0 patch" and 2f0da72721b1's OpenSSL 4 compatibility; the
# ">= 0x40000000L" guards are OpenSSL *4* anticipation -- OpenSSL 3 still takes
# the legacy TLSv1_*_method() path), and the "5.6 + OpenSSL 3 probe" record in
# manifests/SECURITY-REVIEW.md shows the pin compiles and its symbols resolve
# against bookworm's OpenSSL 3 -- but no handshake was tested and
# PHP_OPENSSL_API_VERSION means the OpenSSL 3 provider APIs are never used.
# Evidence only: the route below is unchanged.
#
# PHP_SRC_REPO: all eight series build from this one repository, a fork of
# php/php-src whose per-version branches carry the security backports upstream
# no longer ships for an EOL line. That is why these manifests need no local
# patches/ tree: the patchset IS the pinned branch, not a stack we assemble.
# SOURCE_REF records the release *tag*; PHP_SRC_COMMIT is that tag's peeled
# commit, so the build checks out an immutable object even if the tag were ever
# re-pointed. tools/check-pin-drift.sh reports how far a pin trails upstream.
#
# BUILD_VARIANTS: space-separated list of image variants to produce (cli, fpm).
#
# BUILD_PACKAGES: optional space-separated pinned list of *build-time* packages
# (compiler toolchain + -dev libraries), e.g. "libssl-dev=3.0.15-1~deb12u1".
# Leave empty to use the Dockerfile's per-route defaults. An override replaces
# that whole default list, so it must contain the toolchain and every -dev
# package this version's configure flags need. The openssl CLI is appended
# automatically because the OpenSSL-route assertion depends on it, and the
# effective list is recorded in the image at /opt/php/BUILD-DEPS.
#
# RUNTIME_PACKAGES: optional space-separated pinned list of *runtime* libraries,
# e.g. "libssl1.1=1.1.1w-0+deb11u8 libcurl4 zlib1g libzip4 libmemcached11
# libjpeg62-turbo libpng16-16 libfreetype6 libwebp6 libonig5" for the bullseye
# base this route now expects. bullseye and bookworm both call the memcached
# library libmemcached11 (1.0.18 vs 1.1.4) and differ on libwebp (libwebp6 vs
# libwebp7). Leave empty
# to use the Dockerfile's per-route defaults until you pin versions. binutils
# (readelf), unzip (Composer 2 dists)
# and the openssl CLI are installed unconditionally in the runtime stage, so an
# override only has to list the shared libraries the build linked against.
# Some bases name runtime libraries differently (e.g. libwebp7 vs libwebp6);
# confirm actual runtime package names for the chosen base OS.
#
# COMPOSER_VERSION / COMPOSER_SHA256: optional overrides. Leave both empty to
# use the Dockerfile policy pin for this series -- Composer 1.10.28, the final
# 1.x release (Composer 2.x requires PHP >= 7.2.5). The phar is fetched from an
# immutable installer commit and verified by SHA-256 at build time. If you pin a
# different release, set COMPOSER_SHA256 alongside it.
#
# SHIP_PHP_SOURCE: 1 (default) ships the patched, configured source/build tree
# in /usr/src/php so downstream images can run docker-php-ext-install, exactly
# like the official PHP images. It is the single largest thing in the image.
# Set 0 for a size-sensitive deployment that will never compile an extension
# inside this image; /usr/src/php then holds only a SOURCE-NOT-SHIPPED note and
# verify-runtime records the choice. The .git history and the PHPT test suites
# are removed either way.
#
# PATCHSET_APPROVED=1 here records one human decision: the upstream patchset
# carried by 5.6.40 is accepted as the reviewed unit (see the "Backports
# patchset" record in manifests/SECURITY-REVIEW.md). It is NOT a claim that
# every open CVE in this series was assessed individually here, and it does
# not survive a pin change -- moving PHP_SRC_COMMIT means a new record. The
# gate still does its job: CI refuses to build a manifest nobody approved.

BASE_IMAGE="debian:bullseye-slim@sha256:e5b6442dd2e9684cf5e87d8338b5968f3b348636fc0be6d7850a381e3731a2bd"
OPENSSL_ROUTE="openssl11"
PHP_SRC_REPO="https://github.com/shivammathur/php-src-backports.git"
SOURCE_REF="5.6.40"
PHP_SRC_COMMIT="31b896540a30a5d170797a1171b04dd872b88c5b"
BUILD_VARIANTS="cli fpm"
# Extensions this series ships ON TOP OF the kit baseline (openssl curl mysqli
# pdo_mysql mbstring zip gd zlib mcrypt + PECL apcu redis memcached). Empty =
# baseline only.
#
# mcrypt is not requested here: it is part of the 5.6-7.1 core baseline in the
# Dockerfile's guard step (in core through 7.1; the PECL replacement floors at
# PHP 7.2.0). The trade-off record stands, because the baseline
# decision is what keeps libmcrypt in the image: libmcrypt has had no upstream
# release since 2.5.8 (2007), the extension exposes no AEAD mode, and its cipher
# list includes algorithms that should not be chosen for new work. It is in the
# image because a legacy application calls mcrypt_* and has no other route onto
# a maintained PHP, not because it is a good way to encrypt something -- anything
# writing new crypto should use ext openssl or ext sodium, both of which are
# baseline or reachable without a dead library. The library is linked into the
# image (libmcrypt4 in the runtime stage); removing mcrypt from the baseline is
# a reviewed Dockerfile change that removes that attack surface too. Accepted
# knowingly; the review record is the libmcrypt entry under "Bundled third-party
# components" in manifests/SECURITY-REVIEW.md.
PHP_EXTENSIONS=""
PECL_EXTENSIONS=""
BUILD_PACKAGES=""
SHIP_PHP_SOURCE="1"
RUNTIME_PACKAGES=""
COMPOSER_VERSION=""
COMPOSER_SHA256=""
PATCHSET_APPROVED="1"
